    // The landing area itself, and rung one of decision 0061's ladder: a region stores its extent and
    // nothing else, and wears the material its planet already carries. The extent is pinned to the cap
    // of decision 0017, so the set holds exactly one of it and every region is maximal — which is where
    // a tiled material is hardest on the eye and therefore where the first cycle asks its question.
    { "id": 29, "label": "landing-region", "category": "region",
      "parameters": { "extent": [2048, 2048] } },
    // Biomes. A biome-element says which material a stretch of ground wears and the conditions under which
    // it claims a cell: an elevation band as a signed fraction of the body's own relief amplitude
    // (decision 0070), and whether it is what lies under the sea. A planet's `biomes` palette draws from
    // these by tag, so a rocky body paints from rocky biomes and an ocean body from ocean ones.
    //
    // The bands overlap deliberately. Where two biomes both admit a cell the nearer patch wins, so the
    // boundary between them wanders with the patches instead of following a contour line.
    { "id": 30, "label": "lowland-rock", "category": "biome-element", "tags": ["for-rocky"],
      "parameters": { "surface": ["for-rocky"], "biome-submerged": [false], "biome-elevation-low": [-65535, -65535], "biome-elevation-high": [0, 16000] } },
